Project Title

Model a Linux-Style Directory Hierarchy Using a Tree Data Structure

Problem Statement:

To model a Linux-style directory hierarchy using a tree data structure. Given a directory or file node, the program should:
Print the full path of the given node, starting from the root directory /.
Display all siblings of the given node, which are the other nodes sharing the same parent.
Calculate and display the size of the given node's subtree, including the node itself and all its descendants.
The program demonstrates how a tree data structure can represent a Linux-style file system and perform basic directory operations.

Introduction:

Linux organizes files and directories in a hierarchical structure. This structure starts from the root directory, represented by /.
Every directory can contain files and subdirectories. Therefore, the directory structure can be represented using a tree data structure.
In a tree:
The root is the starting node.
A parent node contains or connects to child nodes.
Sibling nodes share the same parent.
A subtree consists of a node and all its descendants.
This project uses a tree to represent directories and files. It finds the full path of a given node, displays its siblings, and counts the total number of nodes in its subtree.

Short Theory:

What is a Tree?
A tree is a non-linear data structure consisting of nodes connected through edges. It represents hierarchical relationships between elements.

Linux Directory Hierarchy
Example:
/
├── home
│   ├── asmita
│   │   ├── notes.txt
│   │   └── project
│   └── student
├── etc
└── bin

In this hierarchy:

/ is the root.

home, etc, and bin are children of the root.
asmita and student are children of home.
asmita and student are siblings because they share the same parent.
notes.txt and project are siblings because they share the same parent, asmita.

Objectives:

To represent a Linux-style directory structure using a tree.

To find the full path of a specified node.

To identify the siblings of a given node.

To calculate the number of nodes in its subtree.

To understand tree traversal and recursion in C programming.

Algorithm:

Step 1: Start the program.

Step 2: Create a tree containing the root directory and its child directories and files.

Step 3: Accept the name of the target node from the user.

Step 4: Search the tree for the target node while keeping track of its parent.

Step 5: If the node is found, construct its full path by following its parent links back to the root.

Step 6: Display the siblings of the target node by listing the other children of its parent.

Step 7: Calculate the subtree size recursively:
Count the current node as 1.
Visit each child.
Add the subtree size of every child to the total.

Step 8: Display the full path, siblings, and subtree size.

Step 9: If the node is not found, display an appropriate message.

Step 10: Stop the program.

Sample Input:

Enter node name: asmita

Sample Output:

Enter node name: asmita
Full path: /home/asmita
Siblings: student
Subtree size: 3

Another Example:

Suppose the user enters:
Enter node name: home
The output will be:
Enter node name: home
Full path: /home
Siblings: etc bin
Subtree size: 6

Advantages:

Hierarchical representation: A tree naturally represents directories and files.

Efficient path construction: Parent pointers make it easy to move from a node toward the root.

Sibling identification: Nodes sharing the same parent can be identified easily.

Subtree calculation: Recursion provides a simple method for counting descendants.

Dynamic structure: New directory and file nodes can be added.

Educational value: The program demonstrates pointers, structures, recursion, and tree traversal in C.

Disadvantages:

The example uses a fixed directory structure.
Searching by name may take O(N) time.

The program does not create actual directories or files on Linux.

Nodes with identical names in different directories can make name-only searching ambiguous.

Dynamic memory allocated with malloc() is not freed before the program exits.

The program does not implement file permissions, symbolic links, or other real file-system features.

Applications:

This project demonstrates concepts useful in:

Linux file-system modeling: Representing directories and files hierarchically.

Directory navigation: Understanding the relationship between parent and child directories.

File management systems: Representing folders, files, and their relationships.

Tree traversal: Searching and processing hierarchical data.

Disk usage analysis: Counting files and directories within a subtree.

Operating systems education: Learning how hierarchical file systems can be modeled.

Data organization: Representing organizational charts, category trees, and nested structures.

Conclusion:

This project demonstrates how a Linux-style directory hierarchy can be modeled using a tree data structure in C.
The program finds a specified node, prints its full path, identifies its siblings, and calculates the number of nodes in its subtree. It uses structures, pointers, recursion, and tree traversal to perform these operations.
The project helps develop an understanding of hierarchical data representation and basic file-system concepts. It also demonstrates how tree data structures can be applied to directory management, file organization, and operating systems.